      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}+1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(1-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{3}-1)}{(\\sqrt{3}+1)(\\sqrt{3}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-3+\\sqrt{3}}{3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-4}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
